# Set up alert rules

Alert rules watch the runs in a project and tell you when a number crosses a limit. Create them from the Alerts page of a project in the dashboard. Each rule checks one metric over a time window and notifies the destinations you pick.

Uncoil checks every enabled rule on a schedule, about every five minutes. A rule opens an alert the first time it goes over its limit and closes it when the value drops back under. It does not send a new message on every check while it stays over.

## Choose a metric

Every rule uses one of four metrics. A rule fires when the value is greater than its limit.

| Metric | What it measures | Limit is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Failed runs | Runs with a failed status inside the window | A count |
| Cost | Total cost of runs inside the window | US dollars |
| Slow runs (p95) | The duration that 95 percent of runs finish within, inside the window | Seconds |
| Minutes without runs | Time since the most recent run | Minutes |

Cost and duration come from the data on your runs, so they are only as accurate as what you [record on spans](/docs/costs).

## Create a rule

A rule has these settings:

- **Name.** Up to 80 characters. It appears in notifications and history.
- **Metric and limit.** The limit must be zero or more.
- **Window.** Between 5 minutes and 1 day (1440 minutes). Values outside that range are adjusted to fit.
- **Environment.** Optional. When set, only runs from that environment are counted.
- **Destinations.** At least one of email, Slack, or a webhook.

The new-rule form previews what the rule would have done over the last seven days, so you can tune the limit before saving. A project can have up to 25 rules.

## Choose where alerts go

| Destination | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Email | Up to 10 addresses per rule. Duplicates are removed and each address is validated. |
| Slack | A Slack incoming webhook link. It must be an https link on `hooks.slack.com`. |
| Webhook | Any public https address. Local, private, and credential-bearing addresses are refused. |

Slack and webhook destinations also receive a message when the rule recovers. Email is sent when a rule fires.

Use **Send a test** on the form or on a saved rule to post a clearly labelled test message and see whether it was delivered. The dashboard shows the latest delivery result for each Slack and webhook destination.

### Webhook payload

A webhook receives a JSON `POST` with these fields:

```json
{
  "event": "alert.fired",
  "rule": { "id": "...", "name": "Failures", "metric": "failed_runs", "threshold": 5 },
  "value": 7,
  "message": "Failures: 7 failed runs in the last hour (limit 5).",
  "projectId": "...",
  "dashboardUrl": "https://www.uncoil.dev/dashboard/.../alerts",
  "firedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"
}
```

The `event` is `alert.fired`, `alert.resolved`, or `alert.test`. Requests time out after 10 seconds and redirects are not followed.

## Manage rules

- **Enable or disable.** Disabling a rule stops checks and resets it to normal.
- **Snooze.** Mute a rule for up to 7 days. It keeps checking and recording alerts but sends nothing. Wake it early at any time.
- **Quiet hours.** One daily window per project, with a start time, end time, and time zone. Alerts are still recorded, but nothing is sent during the window.
- **Tags.** Up to 8 short labels per rule, lowercase, up to 24 characters each, for grouping and filtering.
- **History.** Every alert is listed with its rule, message, time, and whether it is active or resolved. Alerts held back by a snooze or quiet hours are marked as such.

## Check your setup

1. Create a rule with a low limit and a short window.
2. Press **Send a test** and confirm the message arrives.
3. Raise the limit to a realistic value, and set an environment if you only care about production.
4. Add quiet hours if you do not want overnight messages.

For help reading the runs behind an alert, see [trace anatomy](/docs/trace-anatomy) and [troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).
